{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Randomization.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Randomization.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"randomization randomization","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Randomization.md","fileName":"Randomization.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Randomization","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 14:36\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Game Analytics and Probability]] [[Level and Scene Design]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Analytics and Probability","Level and Scene Design","advancedgamedesign.pdf","agamedesignvocabulary.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Randomization.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Randomization.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"randomization randomization","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Randomization.md","fileName":"Randomization.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Randomization","heading":"Randomization","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Randomization selects among outcomes using apparent chance, through devices such as dice, shuffled cards, or software-generated values. Perfect physical randomness is unnecessary when the result is functionally unpredictable to players.\n\nGames use randomization to represent detail below the chosen modeling level and to create uncertainty. The resulting probabilities must still support perceived fairness, because players often judge short sequences by intuition rather than by statistical expectation.\n\nIn scene design, chance is most useful for generating combinations among already meaningful rules. It can prevent memorization, break stagnation, and force competitors away from symmetrical strategies, but it is not a substitute for designing strong interactions.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Analytics and Probability","Level and Scene Design","adva